One TypeScript source for Deno and Bun: `@pancake/stack`
([`mod.ts`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/ts/stack/mod.ts))
plus optional `@pancake/iroh`. `Session` / `Server` over `libpancake_driver`;
`connect` loads iroh lazily. Build archives with `zig build` (iroh tabs need
`zig build iroh-abi`), set `PANCAKE_LIB_DIR`, run with
`deno run --allow-ffi --allow-read` (add `--allow-net` / `--allow-env` as
needed). Binding check: `deno-dogfood` / `bun-dogfood`. Install details:
[Bindings]($link.page('bindings')).

## The same round-trip

The [Start building]($link.page('start')) round-trip (connect, fetch, send,
handle the reply) reads like this in TypeScript. Start `serve` yourself (Rust
auto-serves on `connect`); `fetch` / `send` also start the inbound loop on
demand.

> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **tcp**
> ```ts
> import { connect, tcpLocator } from "@pancake/stack";
>
> // tcp: the peer is a place, a cleartext host and port.
> const partner = tcpLocator("127.0.0.1", 9099);
>
> const session = await connect(partner);
> session.serve().catch(() => {});
>
> const greeter = await session.fetch("greeter"); // a capability, by name
> try {
>   const reply = await session.send(greeter, "greet", ["world"]);
>   console.log(reply);
> } catch (reason) {
>   console.error("the greeter refused:", reason);
> }
>
> session.close();
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```ts
> import { connect } from "@pancake/stack";
> import { Reachability } from "@pancake/iroh";
>
> // iroh reachability: the peer is a key, not a place. Discovery resolves
> // the bare node id over the open internet; no address in hand.
> const partner = "ocapn://3b6a…da29.iroh";
>
> const session = await connect(partner, Reachability.Discovery);
> session.serve().catch(() => {});
>
> const greeter = await session.fetch("greeter"); // a capability, by name
> try {
>   const reply = await session.send(greeter, "greet", ["world"]);
>   console.log(reply);
> } catch (reason) {
>   console.error("the greeter refused:", reason);
> }
>
> session.close();
> ```

The iroh tab is a bare node id plus posture; fetch/send match the tcp tab.
Dial-by-id and discovery: [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')).

The `serve` promise resolves when the session closes cleanly and rejects on a
transport or protocol error. When the loop ends, every `send` still awaiting a
reply breaks too. Failures surface on the calls you await; the bare `catch`
only keeps the loop's own rejection from going unhandled.

## Hosting the other half

The hosting side reads the same way. `listen` binds a port and returns a
`Server` you iterate for each accepted `Session`, `publish` makes an actor
fetchable by name, and `serve` dispatches the partner's calls to it.

> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **tcp**
> ```ts
> import { listen } from "@pancake/stack";
>
> // tcp: bind a place you chose, a host and port.
> const server = listen("127.0.0.1", 9099);
> for await (const session of server) {
>   session.publish("greeter", (_verb, args) => `hello, ${args[0]}`);
>   session.serve().catch(() => {});
> }
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```ts
> import { listenIroh, Reachability } from "@pancake/iroh";
>
> // iroh reachability: bind a key, not a place. The endpoint publishes its
> // identity, and partners dial the id from anywhere.
> const server = listenIroh(Reachability.Discovery);
> console.log(server.uri); // ocapn://<node-id>.iroh?…; hand this to partners
> for await (const session of server) {
>   session.publish("greeter", (_verb, args) => `hello, ${args[0]}`);
>   session.serve().catch(() => {});
> }
> ```

Over iroh there is no host and port to bind: `listenIroh` takes the posture,
and the server tells you its own locator through `server.uri`.

## Async

Native `Promise`s and `using` for session cleanup. Start the inbound loop with
`session.serve()` (unlike Rust, where `connect` already serves). CapTP
pipelining: [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')).

## Actors

`(verb, args) => AppValue` on `session.spawn` / `session.publish`.
`Host.publishGiftable` is host-wide for three-party gifts. A throw becomes the
caller's broken promise.

## Binding check and example

```sh
zig build deno-dogfood    # the Deno consumer against the pancake archives
zig build bun-dogfood     # the same source, on Bun
```

The full surface lives in the
[TypeScript binding README](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/ts/README.md),
and the
[rooms chat client](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/rooms/deno/lib/room_client.ts)
builds a small capability-secure room on this surface.
